    "caption": "Yves Klein sold empty space. In 1959, Klein began selling zones of empty space. He called them Zones of Immaterial Pictorial Sensibility. Buyers paid for each zone in gold. One zone sold for 20 grams of gold. Each buyer got a signed receipt that looked like a banker's check. To really own the zone, the buyer had to burn the receipt. Then Klein threw half of the gold into the Seine. The ritual needed a museum director and at least two witnesses. He kept the other half for his gold leaf works. In 1962, one ritual was held at the Pont au Double. Photo: dronepicr, CC BY 2.0.",
